WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 269: Elizabeth Cady Stanton: The Declaration of Sentiments, Seneca Falls, and the Solitude of Self

1
00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:05.760
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:07.040
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:15.120
[Rebecca] Today, our top 50 famous non-president American series explores the philosophical fountainhead of the women's rights movement.

4
00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:22.880
[Rebecca] The radical intellectual and mother of seven who demanded the ballot at Seneca Falls and redefined the meaning of human autonomy.

5
00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:29.040
[Rebecca] Here is George with the life of Elizabeth Cady Stanton.

6
00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:42.320
[George] Born in November 1815 into wealth and legal prestige in Johnstown, New York, Elizabeth Cady was the eighth of eleven children born to Daniel Cady, a prominent judge and conservative state congressman.

7
00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:49.760
[George] From her earliest childhood, Elizabeth was exposed to the brutal legal subjugation of women under English common law.

8
00:00:49.760 --> 00:01:00.400
[George] Sitting quietly in her father's law office, she watched weeping widows and wives discover that under the legal doctrine of coverture, marriage legally extinguished a woman's existence.

9
00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:06.640
[George] Transferring all her property, wages, and custody of her own children entirely to her husband.

10
00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:16.320
[George] Elizabeth was so horrified that she took a pair of scissors and resolved to cut every unjust law out of her father's leather-bound legal statutes until her father stopped her.

11
00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:24.400
[George] When her beloved older brother Elizar, the only surviving son in the family, died suddenly after graduating from Union College.

12
00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:34.080
[George] Oh, my daughter, I wish you were a boy.

13
00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:37.040
[George] Elizabeth threw her arms around him and vowed.

14
00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:39.520
[George] I will try to be all my brother was.

15
00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:48.880
[George] Mastering Greek, Latin, Mathematics, and horseback riding at the Johnston Academy, Elizabeth won academic honors above all male classmates.

16
00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:53.1000
[George] Graduating from Emma Willard's Troy Female Seminary in 1832.

17
00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:07.320
[George] Her father Daniel Cady was one of the wealthiest jurists in New York, and his spacious law library became Elizabeth's childhood sanctuary, where she spent hours reading Blackstone's commentaries and observing client consultations.

18
00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:18.200
[George] Witnessing tearful weeping widows being stripped of their homes, family heirlooms, and children by greedy brothers-in-law because common law gave married women no legal existence.

19
00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:25.880
[George] Young Elizabeth once resolved to take a pair of scissors and cut every unjust law out of her father's leather-bound legal codes.

20
00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:37.160
[George] In 1840, Elizabeth married Henry Brewster Stanton, an eloquent, charismatic abolitionist orator, famously insisting on removing the word obey from their wedding vows.

21
00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:42.760
[George] For their honeymoon, the newlyweds sailed to London to attend the World Anti-Slavery Convention.

22
00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:48.920
[George] But upon arrival at Freemasons Hall, the international conference exploded into bitter division.

23
00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:53.960
[George] The male clerical leadership voted overwhelmingly to expel female delegates.

24
00:02:53.960 --> 00:03:00.760
[George] Forbidding women from speaking or voting and forcing them to sit in silence behind a dark heavy curtain in the gallery.

25
00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:05.720
[George] Sitting behind the curtain alongside Philadelphia Quaker matron Lucretia Mott.

26
00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:18.360
[George] Twenty-four-year-old Elizabeth was filled with incandescent rage at the hypocrisy of male abolitionists who fought for the freedom of enslaved black men while silencing the women who financed and sustained their movement.

27
00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:29.320
[George] Walking together through London streets, Stanton and Mott resolved that upon their return to America, they would organize the world's first public convention dedicated to the rights of women.

28
00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:47.120
[George] At the World Anti-Slavery Convention at Freemasons Hall in London, William Lloyd Garrison and Nathaniel Peabody Rogers demonstrated solidarity with the excluded American women by refusing to sit on the floor of the convention, choosing instead to join Stanton and Mott in the curtained visitors' gallery.

29
00:03:47.360 --> 00:04:04.720
[George] Walking through the streets of London with Lucretia Mott, 24-year-old Elizabeth discovered her life's true calling, pledging to organize an independent national convention upon their return to America, dedicated exclusively to the social, civil, and religious rights of womanhood.

30
00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:16.960
[George] Settling in the small industrial town of Seneca Falls in upstate New York, Stanton found herself isolated, overwhelmed by domestic housework and raising a growing family of young boys.

31
00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:30.960
[George] In July 1848, gathering for tea at the home of Jane Hunt in nearby Waterloo with Lucretia Mott, Stanton poured out her pent-up discontent against the soul-crushing drudgery and legal inferiority imposed on women.

32
00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:37.280
[George] Acting swiftly, they placed an anonymous notice in the Seneca County courier announcing a convention.

33
00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:45.600
[George] Drafting the convention's charter on a small mahogany table, Stanton cleverly modeled it on Thomas Jefferson's Declaration of Independence.

34
00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:51.200
[George] We hold these truths to be self-evident, that all men and women are created equal.

35
00:04:51.200 --> 00:05:03.440
[George] Stanton enumerated sixteen grievances against the history of mankind, including taxation without representation, denial of property rights, and exclusion from higher education and professions.

36
00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:11.760
[George] Most radically of all, Stanton included Resolution 9, demanding the sacred, constitutional right to the elective franchise.

37
00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:14.000
[George] Even Lucretia Mott warned her.

38
00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:16.640
[George] Why, Lizzy, the will make us ridiculous.

39
00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:21.680
[George] At the Wesleyan Chapel on July 1920, 300 men and women gathered.

40
00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:29.760
[George] When Resolution 9 was debated, escaped abolitionist hero Frederick Douglass took the floor, delivering a soaring endorsement.

41
00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:33.320
[George] Right is of no sex, truth is of no color.

42
00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:37.080
[George] The resolution passed, igniting a national firestorm.

43
00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:49.000
[George] Meeting around the mahogany tea table at Jane Hunt's home in Waterloo, New York, the five Quaker women recognized that piecemeal petitions were inadequate to address centuries of systemic oppression.

44
00:05:49.000 --> 00:06:12.280
[George] Drafting the Declaration of Sentiments upon the rhetorical framework of Thomas Jefferson's 1776 charter, Stanton systematically catalogued sixteen specific grievances, including the denial of property rights, unequal divorce laws, exclusion from higher education and the professions, and woman's absolute civil death under the common law doctrine of coverture.

45
00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:14.360
[George] Following her meeting with Susan B.

46
00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:21.320
[George] Anthony in 1851, Stanton became the supreme intellectual philosopher of the women's rights movement.

47
00:06:21.320 --> 00:06:28.600
[George] Tied to her Seneca Falls home by the demands of raising seven children, Stanton turned her parlor into a command post.

48
00:06:28.600 --> 00:06:37.880
[George] While children played under the table, Stanton wrote radical speeches, drafted legislative petitions, and authored blistering articles late into the night.

49
00:06:37.880 --> 00:06:44.440
[George] When Anthony visited, they sat by the fireplace for days, debating philosophy and strategy.

50
00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:48.040
[George] Stanton focused on the root causes of female oppression.

51
00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:58.520
[George] She campaigned aggressively for New York's landmark Married Women's Property Act of 1860, which granted mothers equal custody rights over their children and ownership of their wages.

52
00:06:58.520 --> 00:07:13.000
[George] She shocked conservative society by advocating for liberalized divorce laws, arguing that marriage was a human legal contract, not a religious sacrament, and that women should have the legal right to escape abusive, alcoholic husbands.

53
00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:24.000
[George] When Stanton read Resolution 9 demanding the right to the elective franchise, even her venerable mentor Lucretia Mott cautioned, Why, Lizzie, thee will make us ridiculous.

54
00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:33.680
[George] But Frederick Douglass, rising with eloquent moral thunder, declared that a man had no right to claim the franchise for himself while denying it to woman.

55
00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:44.880
[George] Douglas argued that without the ballot, woman was a perpetual political ward lacking any peaceful weapon of self-defense, securing the narrow adoption of Resolution 9 by the convention.

56
00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:55.920
[George] Following the Civil War, the abolitionist and women's rights movements fractured over the Fifteenth Amendment, which granted voting rights to black men while omitting any mention of sex.

57
00:07:55.920 --> 00:08:08.960
[George] Abolitionist leaders like Frederick Douglass and Wendell Phillips argued that it was the Negroes' hour, and that insisting on women's suffrage would doom the amendment and leave newly freed slaves defenseless against white terror.

58
00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:20.720
[George] Stanton and Anthony were furious, refusing to support an amendment that enfranchised formerly enslaved and uneducated men while continuing to disenfranchise educated, tax-paying women.

59
00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:41.360
[George] In her anger, Stanton made controversial, racially charged remarks that alienated black allies and fractured the movement into two rival organizations in 1869, the Radical National Woman Suffrage Association led by Stanton and Anthony, and the Moderate American Woman Suffrage Association led by Lucy Stone.

60
00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:49.680
[George] It was the most painful, regrettable rupture of Stanton's career, delaying the unification of the suffrage movement for two decades.

61
00:08:49.680 --> 00:09:05.240
[George] Stanton's spacious home in Seneca Falls served as the intellectual headquarters of the radical women's movement, where Stanton drafted speeches, legislative petitions, and congressional testimony by the light of tallow candles while her seven children slept.

62
00:09:05.240 --> 00:09:18.680
[George] Anthony's logistical mastery translated Stanton's fiery philosophical treatises into grassroots political action, coordinating nationwide petition drives and organizing state conventions across the northern states.

63
00:09:18.680 --> 00:09:33.960
[George] As she grew older, Stanton recognized that political suffrage alone would never liberate women as long as religious theology taught that woman was an inferior afterthought created from Adam's rib, responsible for the original sin and downfall of humanity.

64
00:09:33.960 --> 00:10:02.680
[George] In 1895, at age 80, Stanton published the first volume of the Woman's Bible, a radical, pioneering feminist commentary on biblical scriptures, assembling an international committee of female scholars, Stanton subjected Genesis, Leviticus, and the Epistles of Paul to rigorous literary and historical criticism, demonstrating how patriarchal church fathers had systematically distorted religious texts to justify the subjugation of women.

65
00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:08.760
[George] The book was a sensational international bestseller, but it terrified conservative suffragists.

66
00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:20.200
[George] At the 1896 convention of the National American Woman Suffrage Association, delegates voted to censure and repudiate Stanton's book, fearful it would alienate Christian voters.

67
00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:27.560
[George] Stanton stood tall and unrepentant, remarking that she had outgrown the narrow orthodoxy of the movement she had founded.

68
00:10:27.560 --> 00:10:40.440
[George] Stanton's refusal to compromise her radical philosophical principles often brought her into sharp conflict with more conservative, church-oriented suffragists who sought to keep the movement narrowly focused on voting alone.

69
00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:57.200
[George] Stanton argued passionately that religious orthodoxy, hierarchical marriage customs, and economic dependence were interlocking chains that held woman in perpetual servitude, insisting that true emancipation required the complete dismantling of patriarchal theological dogma.

70
00:10:57.200 --> 00:11:11.360
[George] In 1892, upon resigning the presidency of the National American Women's Suffrage Association at age 76, Elizabeth Cady Stanton delivered her sublime philosophical masterpiece, the Solitude of Self.

71
00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:19.200
[George] Moving far beyond the ballot, Stanton articulated the tragic, majestic existential condition of every human soul.

72
00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:28.480
[George] She argued that because every individual must ultimately face the storms of life, make moral decisions, and enter the mystery of death completely alone.

73
00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:33.840
[George] Every human being must be equipped with full sovereignty, education and autonomy.

74
00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:43.440
[George] No matter how much women prefer to lean, to be protected and supported, nor how much men desire to have them do so, they must make the voyage of life alone.

75
00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:45.840
[George] Such is the solitude of self.

76
00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:59.600
[George] On October 26, 1902, two weeks shy of her 87th birthday, Stanton died quietly in her New York apartment, having written letters to President Theodore Roosevelt urging woman's suffrage the day before.

77
00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:13.200
[George] Elizabeth Cady Stanton was the supreme visionary of human freedom, the woman who looked beyond the ballot to imagine a world where every human soul could walk in the full dignity of intellectual, spiritual, and bodily liberty.

78
00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:16.720
[George] In her masterpiece address, The Solitude of Self.

79
00:12:16.720 --> 00:12:35.480
[George] Delivered before the House Committee on the Judiciary in 1892, Stanton articulated the ultimate philosophical justification for female sovereignty, that every individual's soul must navigate the voyage of life alone, facing life's sorrows, decisions, and mortality in profound solitude.

80
00:12:35.640 --> 00:12:45.320
[George] Therefore, to deny woman the fullest development of her faculties, the highest education, and total political freedom was a crime against human nature itself.

81
00:12:45.320 --> 00:12:51.080
[George] Her revolutionary vision laid the ideological cornerstone of modern human rights.

82
00:12:54.600 --> 00:13:01.480
[Rebecca] Next week, our top 50 famous non president American series continues with episode 270.

83
00:13:01.480 --> 00:13:07.000
[Rebecca] Clara Barton, the Angel of the Battlefield, Antietam, and the American Red Cross.

84
00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:09.400
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

